cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  mirror-tenocyte: hypothesis space and experimental design for
  mixed-chirality collagen-mimetic matrices
message: >-
  This repository contains no measurements. It is an experimental
  design and a record of what is and is not settled. If you use the
  protocol or the hypothesis decomposition, please cite it as
  unpublished software/design, not as a source of results.
type: software
authors:
  - alias: lysyloxidase
repository-code: 'https://github.com/lysyloxidase/mirror-tenocyte'
abstract: >-
  An in silico exploration of how mixed-chirality (D/L) collagen or
  collagen-mimetic peptide matrices would affect tenocyte
  mechanotransduction. The original question — the phenotype of a cell
  in a mirror-image environment — dissolves: integrin recognition is
  stereospecific, so a pure mirror matrix is equivalent to a
  non-adhesive substrate. What remains is a materials question about
  MIXTURES.

  The work decomposes collagen assembly into two levels. Level 1
  (chain to triple helix) is settled: heterochiral triple helices do
  not form, supported both by a molecular-dynamics penalty of
  ~7.87 kcal/mol per D residue and by the experimental finding that a
  single D-Asp prevents triple-helix formation in physiological
  buffer. Level 2 (triple helix to fibril) is unconstrained by any
  published data located here, and admits four distinguishable
  outcomes — self-sorting, growth capping, majority-rules chiral
  amplification, and racemic enhancement — which differ in the SHAPE
  of modulus versus composition rather than only its sign.

  The deliverable is PROTOCOL.md: a bench-ready rheology-plus-CD
  experiment on a D/L collagen-mimetic peptide composition series that
  separates the four, including several ways the design can be got
  wrong. All-D collagen itself is not synthesisable (about 2.8x beyond
  the mirror-image protein synthesis record), which forces the work
  onto collagen-mimetic peptides.

  The repository also documents four claims that were made and then
  retracted, and one transferable methodological check: a
  mechanotransduction ODE cascade built as a monotone chain is
  provably a reparametrisation of fewer variables and cannot
  distinguish upstream mechanism, only magnitude.
keywords:
  - chirality
  - collagen-mimetic peptide
  - self-assembly
  - mechanotransduction
  - experimental design
  - hydrogel rheology
license: MIT
version: 0.2.0
date-released: '2026-09-06'
